      "## The therapeutic frame",
      "Professional boundaries organize:",
      "- The counselor's role and the client's role\n- Purpose and goals of contact\n- Time, place, availability, and communication\n- Fees and business arrangements\n- Confidentiality and records\n- Touch, self-disclosure, gifts, and contact outside sessions\n- Technology and social media\n- Role changes, endings, and future contact",
      "The frame exists because the relationship is fiduciary-like and power-unequal. Clients disclose private information and may depend on the counselor's expertise, judgment, access, evaluation, or documentation. The counselor therefore bears the greater responsibility for maintaining conditions that serve client welfare.",
      "## Extension versus violation",
      "The same action can carry different risk in different contexts. A brief, client-initiated handshake may be ordinary in one setting and harmful in a trauma context. Attending a ceremony in a rural community may support treatment or may expose the counseling relationship. The label comes from the function, context, power, and effect—not from a memorized list alone.",
